# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Festa de Disfresses — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development (`.env.local` is git-ignored):
#     cp .env.example .env.local
# On Vercel, set the same variables under Project → Settings → Environment
# Variables. The app runs without any of them, but with reduced functionality.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED for the admin page (/admin) and the GET /api/admin endpoint.
# Any non-empty string; treat it like a password and use something long.
# When this is unset, /api/admin answers 401 to every request, so the admin
# dashboard stays closed — that is the intended "safe by default" behaviour.
# The key is passed as `?key=...` and is compared in constant time.
ADMIN_KEY=canvia-aquesta-clau

# OPTIONAL locally / STRONGLY RECOMMENDED on Vercel.
# Injected automatically by Vercel once a Blob store is created and connected
# to the project (Vercel dashboard → Storage → Blob → Connect Project); you do
# not need to paste it by hand. Do not commit a real token.
#
#   set   -> storage driver "blob": assignments live in Vercel Blob and survive
#            deployments, cold starts and function restarts.
#   unset -> storage driver "file": assignments live in ./data/assignments.json.
#            Fine for local development. On Vercel the filesystem is ephemeral,
#            so every assignment WILL be lost on the next deploy or cold start.
#
# The live driver is shown in the header of the /admin dashboard.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=vercel_blob_rw_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# OPTIONAL — leave it unset unless you really mean it.
# Hard cap on how many assignments a single client IP may create. Unset (the
# default) means no limit at all; the IP is still recorded and shown in /admin
# so you can eyeball it. Setting it makes POST /api/claim answer
# 429 { "error": "ip_limit" } once an IP is over quota.
#
# WARNING: guests register from home, weeks ahead, so a cap no longer locks out
# the whole guest list the way it would if everyone claimed on the party WiFi.
# It does still refuse real guests: housemates, couples and families share one
# home IP, and anyone claiming over mobile data sits behind carrier-grade NAT
# together with strangers. All of them would get a 429 and no country.
#
# Prefer the "possibles duplicats" panel in /admin: it groups the emails that
# share a device or an IP so you can judge them yourself.
#
# There is deliberately NO MAX_PER_DEVICE. The fd_device cookie is the stronger
# duplicate signal, but a hard block on it would silently lock out a couple
# sharing one laptop — so it only ever reports, never refuses.
# MAX_PER_IP=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL_DNS_CHECK — optional, leave unset in production
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /api/claim looks up the MX records of the email's domain and refuses the
# claim when there are none, which rejects invented domains and parked
# typo-squats like gmial.com. It never blocks on DNS trouble: a SERVFAIL or a
# timeout fails open, because no guest should be left without a country
# because a resolver hiccuped.
#
# It cannot tell you whether the mailbox itself exists, and it cannot tell you
# the guest owns it — the app never sends mail, so ownership is unverifiable
# by design.
#
# Set to "off" only for local testing against made-up domains (@prova.cat and
# friends publish no MX records and are rejected while this is on).
# EMAIL_DNS_CHECK=off
